  "query_preview": "..The lingering remnant moisture stream from the \ndeparting weekend system is yielding some modest cloud cover \nacross the southeastern half of the forecast region, but \nconditions remain dry. Across southern Utah the lack of \nprecipitation and cloud cover resulted in warmer afternoon \ntemperatures than Sunday, while much of the remainder of the area \nhas seen cooler temperatures courtesy of a cold frontal passage. \nWhile a calmer and clearer night will result in cooler \ntemperatures tonight into Tuesday morning, weather is expected to \nremain non-impactful.\n\nThroughout much of the upcoming week the combination of a \nrelatively low amplitude northern stream and a ridge extending \nacross the Desert Southwest will maintain zonal flow atop the \nlocal forecast region. This will help bring about a gradual day to\nday warming trend, with near normal temperatures Tuesday \nincreasing to about 5F to 10F above normal by around Thursday. \nSlight moisture intrusions around the periphery of the ridge will \nbring about periods of some cloud cover, but there is little in \nthe way to suggest appreciable precipitation chances through at \nleast Friday. Even then, there is not enough consensus in models \nto include much more than some isolated shower and thunderstorm \nchances across portions of southern Utah during the afternoon.\n\nThe upcoming weekend still appears to be the most likely window \nfor passage of the next potential system, but confidence is still \nquite low at this point. Generally, models suggest any such system\nwould be more of a northern stream trough, which brings questions\non how much moisture would be available. Even looking at the \nbroader ensemble range for a possible northern Utah cold frontal \npassage, there is a spread of 700 mb temperatures between the \n25th-75th percentile of about 10C, corresponding to an NBM \nforecast 25th-75th percentile Sunday high temperature range at \nKSLC of 66F to 84F. This spread decreases at areas further south \nin Utah where chances start to increase in a grazing type system \nbringing about a more muted cooldown. Looking at broader \nscenarios, around 75% of ensemble members at least carry some sort\nof system/front, with only 25% bringing continued dry and mild \nconditions. Of those bringing a system, members are fairly split \non the strength of the front and degree/extent of a cooldown, and \nif/how much precipitation will come from it.",
